--- !u!114 &1754034719
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1754034716}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5f7201a12d95ffc409449d95f23cf332, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_FontData:
    m_Font: {fileID: 10102, guid: 0000000000000000e000000000000000, type: 0}
    m_FontSize: 14
    m_FontStyle: 0
    m_BestFit: 0
    m_MinSize: 10
    m_MaxSize: 40
    m_Alignment: 0
    m_AlignByGeometry: 0
    m_RichText: 1
    m_HorizontalOverflow: 0
    m_VerticalOverflow: 0
    m_LineSpacing: 1
  m_Text: "<b><color=#ffff00>Wardrobe</color></b>\n\n<i>A DynamicCharacterAvatar
    can be of any Race or Sex, and can be changed on the fly. The Wardrobe specifies
    what the avatar is wearing. The Wardrobe Slots are defined on the race. The wardrobe
    not only contains clothing, but also face overlays, complexion, hair, etc. The
    system manages the wardrobe so the avatar only has one wardrobe item per slot.\n\nWardrobe
    parts are simply recipes. They can contain as many UMA slots and overlays as
    needed, and these are all merged into the base race recipe as needed.\n</i>\n\nIn
    this sample code:\n\nThe <b><color=#ffff88>wardrobe</color></b> is changed in
    the \"WardrobeHandler\" class:\n\n        Avatar.SetSlot(Recipe);\r                     
    // This is your precreated slot recipe\n        Avatar.BuildCharacter();\r                    
    // this rebuilds the recipe for the character\n        Avatar.ForceUpdate(true,
    true, true); // this rebuilds the UMA avatar from the recipe"
